  "state": {
    "case_id": "79",
    "case_summary": "A 58-year-old woman experienced a one-day episode of melena and light-headedness. She had no known co-morbidities. Despite being anemic, she was hemodynamically stable, and her general physical and abdominal examinations were unremarkable. Laboratory tests showed a hemoglobin level of 7.3 g/dl with a normal clotting profile. After initial resuscitation, an esophagogastroduodenoscopy (OGD) was conducted, which ruled out any upper gastrointestinal bleeding. A subsequent colonoscopy confirmed the presence of blood in the gastrointestinal tract but did not identify the bleeding site. The patient's hemoglobin level continued to decrease over the next two days, necessitating a transfusion of six units of packed red cells.\n\nSelective superior mesenteric angiography demonstrated a large hyper vascular mass with an extensive contrast blush, near the terminal ileum in the right iliac fossa. It was supplied by a large feeding artery and drained via a solitary large vein into the portal venous system. No extravasation of contrast was seen. Inferior mesenteric and coeliac axis angiography revealed no synchronous lesion or evidence of any vascular deposit in the liver. The histopathology of the resected tumor showed a submucosal tumor compressing the mucosal lining causing ulceration. Sectional analysis of the tumour showed thick bands of muscle with vascular spongy stroma. Microscopic examination showed thick bands of smooth muscle staining positive for smooth muscle antigens using immunochemistry. Immunochemistry was negative for gastrointestinal stromal tumors (C-Kit antigens) and sarcomas. Interspersed between these muscle fibers were a variety of vascular channels ranging in size from dilated capillaries to a small and medium sized arteries and veins. The smooth muscle appeared to arise from the muscularis propria of the small bowel. There was no significant mitotic activity in the tumor and no evidence of tumor necrosis. The tumor was well circumscribed with clear excision margins.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
